      "textEn": "62\n\nIt happens, which is tormented by ghosts. Therefore, man should understand the stable position of the soul and\nwant to know how he is taken by the waves of nature, under sorrow and desire, to various\nbodies and different positions. A person attains success in life when he\nunderstands his natural state and does not deviate from the circumstances created by nature\n(Prakrutih kriyamanani gunai karmani sarvashah). And, by the qualities, the one who is restless in mind. Yati tatsamatyam bhadre halyangalo lingivarva. 24. Meaning of the word\n\nEvam—in this way; Gunaih—by the qualities of nature; Bhramyamane—when distracted; Manasi—in the mind; Avikalah—without change; Puman—living being; Yati—goes; Tat-samyatam—a state like the disturbance of the mind; Bhadre—Oh, Bhadra mother; Hi—certainly; Alingah—without subtle or gross body; Ling-van—possessed of a physical body; Iva—as if.\nSimilarly, O my noble mother, when this mind is distracted by the movement of the qualities of nature,\n(wanders) then the soul, no matter how subtle or gross, from the various states of the body,\neven if it is free, it thinks that it has been transformed from one state to another. Meaning: As it is said in Shrimad Bhagavatam (10.84.13)—\n\nYasyatmabuddhih kunape tridhatuke\nSwadhih kalatraadishu bhaumijyadhih. He who is wise in the pilgrimage, does not do anything in the rain.\nHe is the one who knows the knowledge of the world. “The man who recognizes the body made of three elements as the soul, who considers his body as his own, who considers his birthplace as sacred, and who, instead of meeting divine wise men in pilgrimage,\ngoes to bathe, should be considered as a cow or a donkey.\" Although\nHiranyakashipu was a great demon, but he was not as foolish as today's public.\nHe had clear knowledge of the soul and subtle\nand gross bodies, but today we have become so fallen that every person, including great scientists,\nphilosophers, and leaders, is subject to bodily intellect, which has been condemned by the scriptures. S\nEv Gokharah—Such people are equal to cows and donkeys."
