      "textEn": "66\n\nWhen the queens cried in a loud voice, their tears rolled down their chests, where they turned red with kumkum powder,\nand then fell on the lotus feet of their husband. Their hair fell out, their\nornaments fell off, and the queens, awakening sympathy from the hearts of others,\nbegan to mourn their husband's death. Oh, by the compassion of the creator, O Lord,\nYou have made the ten visible objects manifest. The one who is the cause of the old tradition of the womb,\nHe who has now made the womb of the virtuous. 33. Meaning of the word\n\nAho—Oh; Vidhatra—by the creator; Akarunena—extremely cruel; Nah—our; Prabho—Oh Lord; Bhavan—You; Pranitah—entrusted;\nChinaha—seized; Drishtik—of sight; Agocharam—outside the boundary; Dasham—of the state; Ushinaranam—to the residents of Ushinara;\nAsi—was; Vritti-dah—giving livelihood; Pura—before; Kritah—completed; Adhuna—now; Yena—by which; Shucham—to sorrow;\nVivardhanah—increasing. Oh Lord, now you have been hidden from our sight by the cruel creator.\nBefore this, you\nprovided livelihood to the residents of Ushiner, which made them happy, but now your condition\nhas become the cause of their sorrow. With gratitude, we are your servants, O King.\nWithout whom, we have no good fortune. There, by your heroic feet,\nWherever you go, she listens to the needs of those who serve. 34). Meaning of the word\n\nTvaya—you; Kritagnena—of a very grateful person; Vayam—we; Maahe—to the king; Katham—how; Vina—without; Sayam—\nwill remain; Suhrut-tamena—the best among our friends; Te—yours; Tatra—there; Anuyanam—while following; Tava—yours; Veera—O brave\nman; Padayooha—of the lotus feet; Shushrushatinam—of those engaged in service; Disha—Please give the order; Yatra—where;\nYasyasi—will go. Oh king, oh brave one, you were a very grateful husband and a very loyal friend to all of us. How can we live without you? Hey Veer, wherever you are going, please guide us.\nSo that we can follow your footsteps and serve you again. Let us\nalso take with us.\nMeaning: In ancient times, a Kshatriya king generally had many wives, and the king's"
