      "textEn": "68\n\nWas and was thinking for some reason that if the body remains, then her husband will stay with her. Such a belief certainly exists for cows and donkeys. We have actually\nseen that sometimes when a cow's calf dies, the cowherd presents the dead body of that calf\nby placing it and deceiving the cow. In this way, that cow which otherwise does not give milk,\nlicking the dead body of the calf, gives milk. This confirms the description of the scriptures that\na foolish man is like a cow due to his body-consciousness. Not only foolish people and women consider the body\nas the soul, but we have seen that the corpse of a so-called Yogi was kept by his disciples for several days\nbecause they were thinking that their guru is in Samadhi. When the body started to rot,\nand a foul smell started coming, then somewhere the disciples allowed the so-called Yogi's body to be burned.\nIn this\nway, in foolish people, the body-consciousness is extremely strong, those who are compared to cows and donkeys\nare. Nowadays, big scientists are in the process of refrigerating dead bodies so that in the future these\nrefrigerated bodies can be revived. This historical account described by Hiranyakashipu\nmust have happened millions of years ago, because he lived millions of years ago and even then he was giving an example of history. Therefore, this event must have happened before the lifetime of Hiranyakashipu, but\nthe same ignorance of bodily consciousness is still prevalent not only among the ignorant but also among scientists, who\nthink about reviving frozen corpses. It seems that the queens were not giving the corpse to be burned because they were afraid of dying with their husband's\ncorpse. There, the ghosts, hearing the tears, were distressed.\nAh, having become Tambalako, Yama himself approached. 36. Meaning of the word\n\n[^1]: Tatra—there; ha—certainly; prita-bandhunam—of the friends and relatives of the dead king; ashritya—hearing; pariveditam—a sharp l