      "textEn": "71\n\nWhether you accept it or not, it is futile to grieve for the loss of the body. It is said in the Mahabharata—Adarshanad Ihaayatah Punashchaadarshanam Gataha. This statement supports the theory of atheistic\nscientists that the fetus in the mother's womb does not have life and it is like a\nbody. According to this principle, if a lump of any substance is removed by surgery,\nthen the life of the organism is not killed. The baby's body is like a tumor.\nAnd if this tumor is surgically cut out and removed,\nand thrown away, then there is no sin in it. This same argument can also be given in the context of kings and queens. The king's body was manifested from an unmanifested source, and now he had attained the unmanifested state from the manifested.\nBecause the expression (abhivyakti) exists only between two unexpressed things,\nthen why did anyone cry for the body that appeared in this gap? Oh, we are blessed here\nOh, we are blessed here\nWe have abandoned our fathers, we do not worry. The unprotected, helpless, and weak,\nHe who is protected, is protected in the womb. 38. Meaning of the word:\n\nAho—Oh; Vayam—We all; Dhanya-tamah—Extremely fortunate; Yat—Because; Atra—At this time; Tyaktah—Unprotected,\nLeft alone; Pitrabhyam—By both father and mother; Na—Not; Vichintayamah—Thinking; Abhakhyamanah—Not eaten;\nJaakar—Having gone; Abalaah—Extremely weak; Vrik-adibhih—By wolves and other ferocious animals; Sah—He (God); Rakshitah—Will protect; Rakshati—Will protect; Yah—He; Hi—Indeed; Garbhe—Inside the womb.\nHow surprising it is that these elderly women do not have a higher life-\nunderstanding like us! Undoubtedly, we are extremely fortunate because although we are children and have been left unprotected by our\nparents to struggle for life, and although we are extremely weak, even then, ferocious animals neither ate us nor destroyed us. In this way, we have firm\nbelief that the God who has provided us protection even in the womb of the mother, He alone will continue to protect us everywhere. Meaning: As it is said in the Bhagavad Gita (18.61)—Ishwarah sarvabhutanam hrdaye—Arjuna, God resides in the heart of every living being. In this way, God protects everyone and their"
