      "textEn": "74.\nImplication: They are the best example of God. Our plans to protect or destroy\ndo not succeed, but whatever God thinks of doing, it actually happens. In this context, the examples given are practical. Everyone has had such experiences. In addition to this, there are many\nclear examples. For example, Prahlad Maharaj said that truly, a child remains dependent on his parents,\nbut even while being with them, the child has to endure many sufferings. Sometimes,\neven with the best medicine and the most experienced doctor, a patient cannot be saved. Since everything depends on God's free will, therefore our only duty is to seek their refuge\nand find their shelter. The beings are not destroyed by their own actions,\nbut they are not created by time.\nThere, the self is situated even in its own nature,\nBy its own qualities, the supreme is bound. 41. Meaning of the word\n\nBhutani—all the bodies of living beings; Taithi—each of their own; Nija-yoni—by their own bodies; Karmabhi—by past deeds;\nBhavanti—are manifested; Kale—in due course; Nabhanti—are invisible; Sarvashah—in every way; Na—not; Tatra—there; Ha—\nUndoubtedly; Atma—soul; Prakruti—within this material world; Api—even though; Sthitah—being situated; Tasyah—of that (physical\npower); Gunaih—by various qualities; Anyatamah—extremely different; Hi—undoubtedly; Baddhate—are bound; Every bound soul gets a different type of body according to its actions, and when its\nwork is completed, then the body also gets destroyed. Although the soul remains in various wombs, in various\ntypes of subtle and gross bodies, but it is not bound by them, because it\nis always considered completely different from the expressed body.\nMeaning: Here it is clearly stated that God is not responsible for the various bodies assumed by living beings. The soul has to assume a body according to the laws of nature and its own actions.\nIt has to. Therefore, it is a Vedic command that individuals engaged in physical tasks 