      "textEn": "75\n\nIf we take advantage, even while being bound by the laws of nature, we can be free to attain our original state.\n(Mameva ye prapadyante maayametaam taranti te). We should have firm belief that God is the best.\nAnd if we take refuge in Him, then He, taking our responsibility, will be able to tell us how\nby leaving physical life, we can return to the abode of God. Without such refuge, a man\nhas to adopt a body according to his actions—sometimes of an animal, and sometimes of a deity, etc.\netc. Although the body is found and destroyed in due time, the soul is actually found in the body.\nNot, but is subject to that particular quality, by which it remains connected in a sinful manner. With spiritual education, a person's consciousness changes, due to which he follows the commands of God\nand becomes free from the influence of the qualities of nature. This body is of a man, deluded.\nJust as a house is built separately.\nThose who are engaged in worldly activities,\nwill be destroyed by time, when they are ruined. 42. Meaning of the word\n\nIdam—This; Shariram—of the body; Purushasya—of the bound soul; Moh-jam—produced by ignorance; Yatha—as; Prithak—separate;\nBhautikam—material; Iyate—is seen; Griham—of the house; Yatha—as; Udakaih—from water; Parthiva—from earth;\nTaajasaih—likewise from fire; Janaḥ—of the bound soul; Kalena—by time; Jātaḥ—produced; Vikṛtah—distorted; Vinashyati—destroys\nGoes. Just as a householder, while being separate from his home, considers his home as inseparable from himself,\nin the same way, due to ignorance, a bound soul considers this body as the soul, although the body\nis actually different from the soul. This body is obtained by the combination of parts of earth, water, and fire.\nAnd when they are transformed in time, the body is destroyed. The soul has nothing to do with this creation and dissolution of the body. Meaning: we pass from one body to another.\nThese bodies are born from our attachment, but\nsouls exist separate from the material bound life. Here, an example is given that a house or\na motorcar is always separate from its owner, but a bound soul, due to attachment, considers it inseparable from itself. A motorcar or house is really made up of physical elements. Until the physical elements are in the right way"
