      "textEn": "77\n\nSuch a wise man should not sit idly, the fool should not grieve. He who is not seen by the listener or the speaker, is not seen anywhere. 44). Meaning of the word\n\nShyagnya: A king named Shyagnya; Nanu: Without doubt; Ayam: This; Shet: Is lying down; Mudhaah: These foolish people; Yam: To whom;\nAnushochatha: You are crying; Yah: Who; Shrotah: Listener; Yah: Who; Anuvakta: Speaker; Iha: In this world; Sahah: He;\nNa: Not; Drishyet: Is visible; Karhichit: At any time..\nYamraj further said: O mourners, all of you are fools. The person for whom you are grieving,\nthe grief you are expressing, is still lying before you. He didn't disappear. Then\nWhat is the reason for your grief? Earlier he used to listen to your words and answer, but you\npeople are now grieving not finding him. This is opposing behavior, because you actually\nhad never seen that person inside the body, who used to listen to you and answer. You\ndo not need to grieve, because the body you have always seen is\nlying before you.\nMeaning: This teaching given by Yamraj as a child is understandable even to a common person. The common person who considers the body as the soul is certainly like an animal.\n(Yasyatmabuddhih kunape tridhatuke...sava gokarah) But even a common person can understand that\nafter death, a human being departs. Although the body remains the same, relatives of the dead mourn\nthat he has passed away because a normal person sees the body, but does not see the soul. As it is described in the Bhagavad Gita—'Dehinoasmi yatha dehe'—the owner of the body, the soul, resides within. After death, when the breath stops going inside the nostrils, then people can understand that\nthe person who was still listening and answering inside the body has now left. Therefore, in reality,\nthe common person concludes that the soul was truly different from the body and has now departed.\nIn this way, even a common person, while being conscious, can know that the real person\nwho was inside the body and was answering after listening, was never seen. Therefore, for someone who has never been seen,\nwhat is the need to grieve?"
